{:edb/db-before-t 1990 :edb/db-after-t 1991 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535303 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:39:55.371Z" 13194139535303 true] [17592186179536 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186179536 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $[MATH: t \\in [0,2\\pi).]$" 13194139535303 false] [17592186179536 1145 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad y = 4\\sin t\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= y - x = 4(\\sin t - \\cos t), \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= - (x + y) =-4(\\cos t + \\sin t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\cos t) =-4\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\sin t) = 4\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(4(\\sin t - \\cos t)) \\cdot (4\\cos t) - (-4(\\cos t + \\sin t)) \\cdot (-4\\sin t)dt \\\\\n&= 16∫_{0}^{2π}\\sin t\\cos t - \\cos^{2} t - \\sin t\\cos t - \\sin^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= 16∫_{0}^{2π}(-1)dt \\\\\n&=-16t \\mid _{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&=-16(2π - 0) \\\\\n&=-32π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 false] [17592186179536 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186179537 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9$." 13194139535303 false] [17592186179537 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186179537 1145 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$, respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad y = 3\\sin t\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= xy = 3\\sin t\\cos t, \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= x^{2} = \\cos^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t) =-\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t) = 3\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(3\\sin t\\cos t) \\cdot (3\\cos t) - (\\cos^{2} t) \\cdot (-\\sin t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}9\\sin t\\cos^{2} t + \\sin t\\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}10\\sin t\\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= - ∫_{1}^{1}10u^{2}du \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 false] [17592186179537 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186179538 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139535303 true] [17592186179538 1100 "The curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right)$. Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C$, measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3716/Source/Images/3c3cd3d66ea52e21e2fe90aa0d9971f2.png)" 13194139535303 false] [17592186179538 1145 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula,\n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$, and\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$.\n\nParameterizing the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{t^2}{4}, \\qquad t\\in \\left[0,4\\right]\n$$\n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4)$. Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T$, so we can apply the above formula for the flux.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3716/Source/Images/e4d6ca89f594b3d7ac45d34d2fda4a6b.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= -2, \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= x = t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t) = 1, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{t^{2}}{4}) = \\frac{t}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∫_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}(-2) \\cdot (\\frac{t}{2}) - (t) \\cdot (1)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4} - 2tdt \\\\\n&=-t^{2} \\mid _{0}^{4} \\\\\n&=-16 - 0 \\\\\n&=-16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 false] [17592186179538 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (\\frac{t}{2})-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186179674 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342787 13194139535303 true] [17592186179674 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342794 13194139535303 true] [17592186179674 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342815 13194139535303 true] [17592186179674 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342822 13194139535303 true] [17592186179674 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342829 13194139535303 true] [17592186179675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342801 13194139535303 true] [17592186179675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342808 13194139535303 true] [17592186179675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342836 13194139535303 true] [17592186179675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342843 13194139535303 true] [17592186179675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342850 13194139535303 true] [17592186179676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342857 13194139535303 true] [17592186179676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342864 13194139535303 true] [17592186179676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342871 13194139535303 true] [17592186179676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342878 13194139535303 true] [17592186179970 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535303 true] [17592186179970 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/27fe7019b715ca8f4d07a3f5e7f6ddc312288a135cb1dcb197aacbb98765cdf9.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by ${y}^{2}=4x$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\,i-4\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139535303 true] [17592186179970 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3716/Source/Images/q-126943.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y^{2} = 4x$ from the point $(0, 0)$ to the point $(1, 2)$. Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = 2i - 4j$ through $C$, measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C$, as shown above." 13194139535303 false] [17592186179970 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2.)$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/b7ac1ce4a79528f5660b8f898739da72489ebe8e8fc156020efae77a103fbec4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2)\\cdot (1)-(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t+{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2+{2}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342787 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342788 13194139535303 true] [17592186342787 1089 "q-127060" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342787 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535303 true] [17592186342787 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342787 1113 #uuid "3cd811a3-e69a-5719-8218-9dc6952b534b" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342787 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}-16{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342789 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342790 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342791 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342792 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342793 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1041 "selection" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342793 13194139535303 true] [17592186342788 1110 #uuid "5976fcb3-aaed-56cc-b56c-e2a8dfaee6f4" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342789 1034 "-\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342789 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535303 true] [17592186342789 1111 #uuid "8d00f716-54f9-5750-bc4d-37042aa8b5a5" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342790 1034 "-\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342790 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535303 true] [17592186342790 1111 #uuid "ca3371ee-c68a-5ea8-8fb9-29d3aa8566be" 13194139535303 true] [1759