$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/0e751adb9f44d24ba89af318fe5f063b1be146bbae45d05fc2dd9a99bef259f2.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3xy=3{t}^{2}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2x+1=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2})(1)-(2t+1)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}-2t-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1-1)-(0-0-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342873 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342874 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342875 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342876 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342877 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1041 "selection" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342876 13194139535303 true] [17592186342872 1110 #uuid "69e1a391-f34a-5ba7-9a6d-24a9629e9a2d" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342873 1034 "2" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535303 true] [17592186342873 1111 #uuid "cad5bf5f-e3ed-5105-8a11-9eb6f4b766ca" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342874 1034 "1" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342874 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535303 true] [17592186342874 1111 #uuid "fb19e00b-d8a1-5d28-8910-c12e82cbeefd" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342875 1034 "0" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342875 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535303 true] [17592186342875 1111 #uuid "bd11cbad-dbb5-5e41-b4c0-f6b9a79528d8" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342876 1034 "-1" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535303 true] [17592186342876 1111 #uuid "9108f1b6-bed0-533e-91f9-7a042c91334d" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342877 1034 "-2" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342877 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535303 true] [17592186342877 1111 #uuid "2b1d3741-52c0-5bfd-89f6-738fd959e621" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342878 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342879 13194139535303 true] [17592186342878 1089 "q-127253" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342878 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535303 true] [17592186342878 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/cd21413d8eea5e2fec67eebe22967c846fd7916fd4fb0fbe5f8efe7ac9df6b1a.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=1-x$ from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\,i+\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139535303 true] [17592186342878 1113 #uuid "2ea662d2-7a30-55b0-b0d0-0769f25e46a7" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342878 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=1-t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,0).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062142/assets/e288ed1095be61e4645fb63c22b7fd2cb605d34795c30557e84afb31b7ec8721.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1)\\cdot (-1)-(1)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342880 13194139535303 true] [17592186342879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342881 13194139535303 true] [17592186342879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342882 13194139535303 true] [17592186342879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342883 13194139535303 true] [17592186342879 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342884 13194139535303 true] [17592186342879 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535303 true] [17592186342879 1041 "selection" 13194139535303 true] [17592186342879 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342884 13194139535303 true] 